 "action_summary": "Rows that existed before the word check answer sentences they say almost nothing about. Measured on the base commit with 2,500 shell requests written by other people (a public corpus, not vendored): 292 reached the point where a row starts, 27 came back pass and 26 of those answered a different request -- 'Print information about all users who are logged in' shares 'all' and 'who' with the python symbol index, source_root is filled with '.', the index of this repository is built and verified, and the door reports pass; three refused sentences had already written a file where the sentence pointed. This change asks a row chosen by the lexical match, before it starts, how much of the request it explains: a content word is explained when the row was matched on it or its own declaration uses it, unexplained when nothing in the row says anything about it, and neither when it is closed-class, a number, a path, quoted, an identifier, a word for where the result goes, or bound as data into one of the row's inputs. When the unexplained words outnumber the explained ones the run is refused before it starts and both lists are named. A typed plan is untouched unless its row opts in with request_word_coverage: majority; the git row does, because its typed family is started by the single word 'history' or 'status' (10 of the 26). Two rows declare words their own test sentences use: git (changed, function, since) and the test selector (run). No capability is added and no row is registered.",
